A||||2||Chance||My HOPEFULL clutch solution||||3||
Z||000000||Chance||11-12-2000||07:00 AM||||After months and months of trying to figure out how to fix my non-shifting 86gt, I was about to give up.  Last night, I put a new slave cyl on, and made sure everything was bled/hooked up right.  Still, it only wanted to shift when the engine was off... While I had the slave out, and some of the shields off, I sprayed some of that Valvoline lube/release agent all over where the slave rod pushes the release lever (because I figure that was what I was going to tear apart tomorrow and wanted the bolt and nut easier to remove) I decided to baby it back to my apartment and not leave it at my parents...well, it was still hard shifting for a while, but then it started shifting easier, and the next thing I know, its shifting from a stop, with the engine running.. ||||reg||3
Z||000001||StuGood||11-12-2000||12:25 PM||||Hope your good luck <B>keeps</B> up  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>By the way, when I read your story, it reminded me of something I read, that talked about lubing the clutch fork bushings.  Might want to give it a read:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/clutch-bushings.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/clutch-bushings.html</A> ||||reg||6
Z||000002||Chance||11-12-2000||01:39 PM||||I read that before I had my clutch replaced about a year and a half ago. I even printed it out and asked the mechanic if he would.  He said he had worked on lots of Fieros, and I would be pleased with the work... Well, for 1200 bucks, I hope so! but I don't think he even picked up the printout or done the extra I asked for when I left...&lt;sigh&gt;  And he was the one who installed the master cylender banjo loop side down. Clutch worked great for about Oh... little over a year and a half  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
